# badge.lgbt @VERSION@

Built from duplico/badge.lgbt @ `@GIT_SHA@`.

Three images, one per target, each in its own directory with everything its
`flash.sh` needs:

    animloader/   badge.lgbt-animloader.hex, cc2640r2f.ccxml, flash.sh
    badge/        badge.lgbt.hex,             cc2640r2f.ccxml, flash.sh
    dongle/       badge.lgbt-dongle.txt,      flash.sh

`manifest.json` lists the same information machine-readably; `flash_all.sh`
at this directory's root reads it to drive the per-target `flash.sh`
scripts in the right order.

## Prerequisites

- A WSL2 shell on a Windows host.
- For the badge and animloader: Code Composer Studio (or a standalone TI
  UniFlash install) on the Windows side, an XDS110 debug probe, and a
  Tag-Connect/cJTAG cable to the badge's header.
- For the dongle: TI MSPFlasher on the Windows side, and an eZ-FET or
  MSP-FET wired for Spy-Bi-Wire (SBWTDIO -> dongle nRST, SBWTCK -> dongle
  TEST, plus 3V3 and GND).
- The probe (including a LaunchPad's onboard XDS110) powers a badge enough
  to flash it, not enough to run it. Flash on probe power, then insert the
  battery.

## Flashing a badge end to end (one command)

    ./flash_all.sh

Flashes the animloader, then the badge firmware, in that order (the order
that matters on a fresh badge: the animloader seeds the SPI flash with the
starting animations before the main firmware runs). Add `-n`/`--dry-run` to
see exactly what it would run without touching a badge;
`--animloader-only`/`--badge-only` to run one half; `--dongle` to also
flash the dongle afterward. `./flash_all.sh --help` for the rest.

It re-seats-and-retries on the two bench failures that cost the most time:
a DSLite `Error -242` and a flashloader timeout, both usually a badly
seated probe rather than a real problem.

## Flashing one target by hand

    cd animloader && ./flash.sh
    cd ../badge && ./flash.sh
    cd ../dongle && ./flash.sh

Each `flash.sh` stages its image to a Windows-native temp path and drives
DSLite (badge/animloader) or MSP430Flasher (dongle); override install
locations with `CCS_ROOT` (default `C:\ti\ccs1271`) or `MSPFLASHER_ROOT`
(default `/mnt/c/ti/MSPFlasher_1.3.20`). Re-run is idempotent.

## If it does not work

- **`Error -242: A router subpath could not be accessed`**, or a
  flashloader timeout partway through a write, almost always means the
  probe is seated badly on the badge's header, not a configuration
  problem -- re-seat before touching anything else.
- **"MSP-FET430UIF is already in use"** or another probe-lock error means a
  zombie debug session holds the probe; a Windows reboot is the reliable
  fix.
- The shipped `cc2640r2f.ccxml` uses probe-supplied power. On a fixture
  that does not supply VCC itself, switch its Power Selection to
  target-supplied and leave the battery in, or flashing fails even with a
  good connection.

The full runbook, including the reasoning behind each of these scripts and
a from-source-build path, lives at `docs/flashing.md` in the
[duplico/badge.lgbt](https://github.com/duplico/badge.lgbt) repository.
